{"name":"Decadent Red","hex":"#8d3630","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"decadent","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Characterized by moral or cultural decline.","Luxuriously self-indulgent."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A person affected by moral decay."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/decadent"},{"term":"red","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The color of the setting sun, blood, and strawberries; the color which is evoked by the longest visible wavelengths (between about 625–740 nm), and a primary additive color.","A revolutionary socialist or (most commonly) a Communist;  a Bolshevik, a supporter of the Bolsheviks in the Russian Civil War."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of a red hue.","Having an orange-brown or orange-blond colour; ginger."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/red"}],"entries":[{"title":"Decadence","blocks":[{"text":"Decadence was a late-19th-century movement emphasizing the need for sensationalism, egocentricity, and bizarre, artificial, perverse, and exotic sensations and experiences. By extension, it may refer to a decline in art, literature, science, technology, and work ethics, or to self-indulgent behavior."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f1/Henryk_Siemiradzki_004.jpeg/330px-Henryk_Siemiradzki_004.jpeg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Decadence"},{"title":"Red","blocks":[{"text":"Red is the color at the long wavelength end of the visible spectrum of light, next to orange and opposite violet. It has a dominant wavelength of approximately 625–750 nanometers. It is a primary color in the RGB color model and a secondary color in the CMYK color model, and is the complementary color of cyan. Reds range from the brilliant yellow-tinged scarlet and vermillion to bluish-red crimson, and vary in shade from the pale red pink to the dark red burgundy."}]